**Theorem 24.2 (all-tag return compensation at every cutoff).** For every integer $k\ge1$ put

$$
d_k=\sum_{r=1}^{k}r2^r,\qquad D_k=2^{d_k}.
\tag{SC.56}
$$

For every $\rho\in\{0,1\}$, every $b\in\{0,1\}^3$ and every integer $S\ge D_k$, there exist retry words $u,v$ and distinct return vectors $\mathbf j,\mathbf j'$ such that

$$
\begin{gathered}
j_3=j'_3=0,\qquad \sum_i j_i=\sum_i j'_i=S,\qquad
|u|_X=|v|_X,\quad |u|_Y=|v|_Y,\\
G_k(h(\rho,b,u,\mathbf j))=G_k(h(\rho,b,v,\mathbf j')).
\end{gathered}
\tag{SC.57}
$$

These are distinct positive histories under every permitted installed $\mu$, with the same $\mathcal B$, total returns, retry multiplicities and full original future law. The words $u,v$ can both be chosen nonempty. Thus no fixed whole raw cutoff recovers even the return vector on the full original interface. The bound $S\ge D_k$ is sufficient; it supplies no bound on the lengths of the compensating retry words.

Proof. Use (SC.25) in the finite-degree integer algebra

$$
\mathcal T_k=\mathbb Z\langle x,y\rangle/(\text{all words of degree greater than }k),
\quad \mathfrak a=1+x,\quad \mathfrak b=1+y,
\quad \Gamma=\langle\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b\rangle,
\quad H=\langle\mathfrak a^2,\mathfrak b^2\rangle.
\tag{SC.58}
$$

Here $\Gamma,H$ are groups of units; let $M$ be the positive monoid generated by $\mathfrak a^2,\mathfrak b^2$, including its identity. Inverses in this proof are finite algebraic expressions, not additional source actions. A positive raw word's coefficient vector in $\mathcal T_k$ is exactly its whole $G_k$.

Let $I$ be the augmentation ideal. Since $I^{k+1}=0$, every $1+z$ with $z\in I$ is a unit. If $g\in1+I^r$ and $h\in1+I^s$, then $gh-hg\in I^{r+s}$ and $[g,h]-1=g^{-1}h^{-1}(gh-hg)\in I^{r+s}$. Thus $[1+I^r,1+I^s]\subseteq1+I^{r+s}$, so the lower central series $\gamma_1=\Gamma$, $\gamma_{r+1}=[\gamma_r,\Gamma]$ ends at $\gamma_{k+1}=1$.

The abelian quotient $\gamma_r/\gamma_{r+1}$ is generated by the at most $2^r$ left-nested weight-$r$ commutators with entries in $\{\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b\}$. The elementary identities $[gh,z]=[g,z]^h[h,z]$ and $[g,hz]=[g,z][g,h]^z$, with $q^h=h^{-1}qh$, prove this by induction: products and inverse entries expand into the generator commutators, and conjugation disappears modulo the next lower central term. The same identities, with the filtration $[\gamma_i,\gamma_j]\subseteq\gamma_{i+j}$, make a weight-$r$ commutator multilinear in its entries modulo $\gamma_{r+1}$. Replacing all its entries by their squares therefore gives its $2^r$-th power modulo $\gamma_{r+1}$. The squared-entry commutator lies in $H\cap\gamma_r$.

Consequently the image of $\gamma_r\cap H\gamma_{r+1}$ in $\gamma_r/\gamma_{r+1}$ contains $2^r$ times every one of those generators. Its index is at most $(2^r)^{2^r}$, without assuming any independence of the commutators. Since each $H\gamma_r$ is a subgroup and

$$
[H\gamma_r:H\gamma_{r+1}]
=[\gamma_r:\gamma_r\cap H\gamma_{r+1}],\qquad
\Gamma=H\gamma_1\supseteq\cdots\supseteq H\gamma_{k+1}=H,
$$

multiplication of the indices proves $[\Gamma:H]\le D_k$. No free-group faithfulness premise is required.

We also need positive representatives, not signed retry words. The signed-generator ball of radius $n\ge1$ in $\Gamma$ has polynomial size. Indeed $\mathfrak a^{-1}=\sum_{i=0}^{k}(-x)^i$ and similarly for $\mathfrak b^{-1}$. In a product of at most $n$ signed generators, the absolute value of each degree-$r$ coefficient is at most $\binom{n+r-1}{r}$: assign its $r$ ordered pattern letters weakly to the $n$ factors, each permitted assignment contributing at most one in absolute value. Hence the ball has at most

$$
\prod_{r=1}^{k}\left(2\binom{n+r-1}{r}+1\right)^{2^r}
\tag{SC.59}
$$

elements. For any $p,q\in M$, the principal left ideals $Mp,Mq$ intersect. Otherwise all $2^n$ products of exactly $n$ factors selected from $p,q$ would be distinct: different last factors give an element in both ideals, while equal last factors cancel and reduce the length. Their signed-generator lengths are at most $n$ times the fixed maximum length of $p,q$, contradicting (SC.59).

It follows that every element of $H$ has the form $m^{-1}n$ with $m,n\in M$. Such fractions contain the positive generators and their inverses and are closed under inversion. For multiplication, if $pb_0=qc_0$ is a common left multiple in $M$, then

$$
(a_0^{-1}b_0)(c_0^{-1}d_0)=(pa_0)^{-1}(qd_0).
$$

This proves closure and the asserted representation, using only positive finite words for the final numerator and denominator.

For $i=0,\ldots,D_k$ let $P_i$ be the Magnus unit of the legal seed-plus-payload word $s_\rho T^ic_{b_1}T^{S-i}c_{b_2}c_{b_3}$. These $D_k+1$ words have equal raw counts, and all displayed exponents are nonnegative because $S\ge D_k$. Two right cosets $HP_i,HP_j$ coincide, with $i\ne j$. Write $P_jP_i^{-1}=m^{-1}n$ using $m,n\in M$; then $nP_i=mP_j$. Represent $n,m$ by actual retry words $u,v$. This is equality of all raw coefficients through degree $k$, not just selected moments. Equality at degree one, together with the equal counts of $P_i,P_j$, forces $|u|_X=|v|_X$ and $|u|_Y=|v|_Y$. If necessary prepend a common $X$ to ensure both retry words are nonempty; equality and these multiplicity relations persist.

Theorem 6.3 proves legality, positivity and distinctness of the resulting histories, since their return vectors differ. Theorem 6.2 supplies the common finite records, and (SC.2) supplies equal acquired counts. From their common $p_4$ phase every legal future raw cylinder $z$ has conditional mass $Z_{A+a_z,B+b_z}/Z_{A,B}$, by the same actual common-prior calculation as (SC.15). The seed and marker records agree as well. Thus all original unfinished cylinders and stopped futures have the same law, with their original deterministic events and delivery; no extra report of an erased past is present. This proves the full assertion. $\square$

